WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.580 --> 00:00:04.340
- Look, you stupid bastard, you've got no arms left!
- Yes, I have.

00:00:04.540 --> 00:00:06.520
- Look!
- Just a flesh wound.

00:00:06.560 --> 00:00:09.660
- In life, when somebody gets injured,
it's just a stroke of bad luck.

00:00:09.680 --> 00:00:11.480
But in movies, it's never an accident.

00:00:11.480 --> 00:00:14.860
An injury's a calculated and meaningful choice
by a writer or director

00:00:14.860 --> 00:00:18.480
to tell us more about a character,
or reinforce certain thematic ideas.

00:00:18.480 --> 00:00:22.859
Each injury, whether it's blindness or becoming handicapped, represents a symbolic handicap

00:00:22.859 --> 00:00:24.320
and who they are as a character.

00:00:24.320 --> 00:00:28.949
And many of the most memorable characters and movie moments were greatly enhanced by this literary concept.

00:00:28.949 --> 00:00:31.200
So let's give some characters a diagnosis.

00:00:31.200 --> 00:00:35.180
- It doesn't hurt you?
- Oh, no. I just keep picking it up and
putting it back in again.

00:00:35.280 --> 00:00:39.260
- First I have to convince you that injuries are much more than just physical in the film.

00:00:39.260 --> 00:00:43.020
So let's say we have a character returning from combat adjusting to civilian life.

00:00:43.020 --> 00:00:45.480
How do we show his inner conflict symbolically?

00:00:45.480 --> 00:00:50.560
Give him a limp. A limp literally hinders his mobility, reflecting his struggle to adapt to this new lifestyle.

00:00:50.560 --> 00:00:54.940
- And I know that your therapist thinks your limp's psychosomatic, quite correctly, I'm afraid.

00:00:54.940 --> 00:00:58.780
- So when he gets the thrill of the chase, and the limp finally goes away, we know what that means.

00:00:59.160 --> 00:01:01.300
- He said you forgot this.

00:01:03.320 --> 00:01:07.440
- The ability to stand and walk normally is a symbol of strength and self-confidence.

00:01:07.450 --> 00:01:11.720
Why do you think the Secret Service didn't allow any pictures taken of FDR in a wheelchair?

00:01:11.720 --> 00:01:14.480
Walking, or the lack of it, makes for a powerful image.

00:01:14.480 --> 00:01:18.300
In Forrest Gump, lieutenant Dan's character arc gets represented through his injuries.

00:01:18.300 --> 00:01:20.660
He wants to die a hero on the battlefield.

00:01:20.660 --> 00:01:22.800
When he fails, he loses the ability to walk.

00:01:22.800 --> 00:01:26.680
His injury directly contrasts Forrest's, who went from handicapped to always running.

00:01:26.700 --> 00:01:31.240
In later scenes, liutenant Dan's frustration is always shown through his reliance on others to move

00:01:31.240 --> 00:01:33.460
because he can't move himself where he wants to be.

00:01:33.600 --> 00:01:37.900
When he finally gets as close as he can to the heavens, challenging the fate he was supposed to have,

00:01:37.900 --> 00:01:40.720
that's when he finally moves on his own.
And eventually...

00:01:40.800 --> 00:01:42.900
[Dan:]
- Yeah, I got new legs

00:01:43.400 --> 00:01:44.560
Custom-made.

00:01:45.220 --> 00:01:46.460
Titanium alloy.

00:01:46.600 --> 00:01:50.720
- The fixing of an injury always reflects the way a character's inner self is being fixed, too.

00:01:50.860 --> 00:01:54.540
If you want a character to have a crutch in their psyche, give them a literal one.

00:01:54.540 --> 00:01:58.880
"There Will Be Blood" starts with eerie scenes depicting the dangerous world of drilling underground.

00:01:59.060 --> 00:02:01.740
Daniel Plainview breaks his leg falling into a well

00:02:01.740 --> 00:02:05.520
and has to crawl all the way from the well in the middle of nowhere to the nearest town.

00:02:05.540 --> 00:02:10.540
Through an injury and how he beats it, we learn about his ruthlessness better than dialogue could ever tell us.

00:02:10.540 --> 00:02:12.900
And in a way, it justifies his actions in the movie.

00:02:12.900 --> 00:02:17.460
[Quentin Tarantino:]
- As much of a bastard as this man
proves himself to be,

00:02:17.740 --> 00:02:18.460
that...

00:02:19.420 --> 00:02:20.460
courage...

00:02:21.140 --> 00:02:28.260
that it would have taken to do that, actually, gives him the heroic right

00:02:28.840 --> 00:02:33.620
for almost everything that he does throughout the rest of the movie.

00:02:33.620 --> 00:02:35.480
- But it doesn't always have to be grandiose.

00:02:35.480 --> 00:02:38.680
You can even give a character a fake injury to play with our expectations.

00:02:43.260 --> 00:02:44.980
[Applause and cheering]

00:02:44.980 --> 00:02:48.840
In "Bonnie and Clyde", Clyde cut off his toe to get out of doing work in prisons,

00:02:48.840 --> 00:02:52.420
and this impairment relates directly to his sexual impotence with Bonnie.

00:02:52.420 --> 00:02:56.980
In literature and film, a physical ailment can often indicate a sexual ailment for a man.

00:02:56.980 --> 00:02:58.960
He's limp in more ways than one.

00:02:59.560 --> 00:03:01.160
- I ain't much of a lover boy.

00:03:01.160 --> 00:03:03.900
- In "Rear Window", Jeff is physically
bound to a wheelchair.

00:03:03.900 --> 00:03:07.260
He doesn't want to marry Lisa, and his physical restrictions from his injury

00:03:07.260 --> 00:03:10.840
compliment his view that he's perhaps being sexually restricted as well

00:03:11.020 --> 00:03:12.380
- And your love life?

00:03:13.220 --> 00:03:14.640
- Not too active.

00:03:14.760 --> 00:03:17.260
- But what about characters like Professor X and Yoda?

00:03:17.260 --> 00:03:21.240
They both have issues with walking, but they're some of the most powerful characters in their worlds.

00:03:21.240 --> 00:03:26.140
I think a lot of mentors have physical handicaps because it reflects how they favor mind over body.

00:03:26.140 --> 00:03:29.800
They've transcended physical limitations to the point where it doesn't reflect the weakness.

00:03:29.820 --> 00:03:31.640
A similar injury is blindness.

00:03:31.640 --> 00:03:35.340
This trope goes as far back as "Oedipus Rex", when the blind man can tell the future,

00:03:35.340 --> 00:03:37.560
while Oedipus is the one seeing things incorrectly.

00:03:37.560 --> 00:03:42.200
In movies, not being tricked by the world around you seems to give you a spiritual wisdom instead.

00:03:42.200 --> 00:03:46.360
Also, have you ever noticed that every superhero has a movie where they lose their powers?

00:03:46.360 --> 00:03:49.700
They can't shoot webs, they don't have their suit, or they get blasted with kryptonite.

00:03:49.700 --> 00:03:52.800
It's because when your life is threatened, or when you're no longer invincible,

00:03:52.800 --> 00:03:54.140
then you become relatable.

00:03:54.140 --> 00:03:55.720
'Cause what's more human than being mortal?

00:03:55.720 --> 00:03:59.220
When two pretty much invincible superheroes fight, it's kind of boring.

00:03:59.220 --> 00:04:01.220
Where are the stakes? How can we relate to them?

00:04:01.220 --> 00:04:04.180
E.T. was such a revolutionary movie,
because for the first time,

00:04:04.180 --> 00:04:07.320
the alien wasn't a giant monster,
or a huge, impenetrable creature.

00:04:07.320 --> 00:04:10.800
He was innocent. And most importantly, he could get sick, making him human.

00:04:10.810 --> 00:04:15.380
I think one of my favorite examples of bringing out humanity through injuries is in "Terminator 2".

00:04:15.380 --> 00:04:17.460
Arnold Schwarzenegger is an emotionless robot,

00:04:17.460 --> 00:04:19.360
but by the end of the movie, he grows on you.

00:04:19.360 --> 00:04:22.380
And it comes from his selflessness and how he lets himself get hurt.

00:04:22.380 --> 00:04:26.420
Even with no sympathetic dialogue, no discernible emotion and a murderous streak,

00:04:26.420 --> 00:04:28.400
the Terminator still gets a hug in the end.

00:04:28.400 --> 00:04:30.700
And we empathize with him because he got hurt.

00:04:30.720 --> 00:04:32.500
[Michael Scott:]
- You know what, here's the thing about "Die Hard 4".

00:04:32.620 --> 00:04:37.080
"Die Hard 1", the original, John McClane
is just this normal guy.

00:04:37.080 --> 00:04:40.100
You know, he's just a normal New York City cop

00:04:40.600 --> 00:04:43.100
who gets his feet cut, he gets beat up.

00:04:43.220 --> 00:04:45.080
But he's an everyday guy.

00:04:45.080 --> 00:04:50.800
In "Die Hard 4", he is jumping a motorcycle into a helicopter in the air.

00:04:51.000 --> 00:04:53.120
You know. He's invincible.

00:04:53.120 --> 00:04:56.240
- At the end of the day, major injuries
remind you of your mortality

00:04:56.240 --> 00:04:58.640
and may make you reconsider what's important to you.

00:04:58.640 --> 00:05:00.280
In film, the same principles apply.

00:05:00.280 --> 00:05:03.840
So the next time somebody gets hurt, remember that it's never just a flesh wound.

00:05:03.960 --> 00:05:06.540
- Come back here and take what's coming to you!

00:05:06.540 --> 00:05:08.320
I'll bite your legs off!

00:05:08.440 --> 00:05:09.480
- Thanks for watching.

00:05:09.800 --> 00:05:13.440
